# Rust Typestate Pattern

The Typestate pattern leverages Rust's type system to enforce state transitions at compile time, improving code correctness and maintainability.

## When to use the Typestate pattern

- When managing data that flows through multiple states in a specific order
- For processes with well-defined state transitions that should be enforced
- To prevent invalid state transitions (e.g., preventing a closed connection from being written to)
- When validating user data that must go through a specific process
- For state machines where compile-time guarantees are valuable
- For enforcing protocols that require operations to happen in a specific sequence
- When implementing builder patterns to ensure all required fields are set
- For resource management with clear lifecycles (open → use → close)

## How the Typestate pattern works in Rust

- Each state is modeled as a distinct type (usually a struct)
- State transition functions take ownership of the current state and return the next state
- The compiler enforces valid transitions through its ownership system
- Old states are invalidated after transitions, preventing invalid operations
- The pattern enforces at compile time that methods can only be called when an object is in the appropriate state

## Core implementation approaches

### 1. Distinct types for states

```rust
struct Stage1 { data: String }
struct Stage2 { data: String }

impl Stage1 {
    fn advance(self) -> Stage2 {
        Stage2 { data: self.data }
    }
}
```

### 2. Generic state parameter

```rust
struct StateMachine<S> {
    data: String,
    _state: PhantomData<S>,
}

// State markers (zero-sized types)
struct StateA;
struct StateB;

impl StateMachine<StateA> {
    fn transition_to_b(self) -> StateMachine<StateB> {
        StateMachine {
            data: self.data,
            _state: PhantomData
        }
    }
}
```

## Implementation guidelines

- Model each state as a separate struct or type
- Design state transition functions that consume one state and produce another
- Always take ownership of states in transition functions to invalidate previous states
- Use method chaining for more ergonomic APIs
- Hide implementation details to prevent users from creating invalid states
- Consider using private constructors with public factory methods
- Document the state flow clearly for API users
- Consider using trait bounds to ensure specific capabilities in each state

## Managing shared data between states

- Use generics with a state type parameter for data that persists across states
- Consider the "type witness" pattern to share data between states
- Use composition to keep shared data while changing state types
- Implement the `From` trait for smooth transitions between states
- Keep state-specific functionality in the appropriate state types
- Use `PhantomData` to track state without runtime overhead

## Typestate pattern examples

- Network connections: Open -> Active -> Closed states
- User data validation: Unvalidated -> Validated -> Processed
- Resource management: Pending -> Acquired -> Released
- Game character states: Standing -> Running -> Jumping
- Form handling: Empty -> Filled -> Validated -> Submitted
- HTTP request building: Missing -> HasUrl -> HasMethod -> Ready

## Combining with the Newtype pattern

The Typestate pattern works excellently with the Newtype pattern:

```rust
// Newtype for HTTP requests
struct RequestBuilder<State>(String, PhantomData<State>);

// State markers
struct Missing;
struct HasUrl;
struct Ready;

impl RequestBuilder<Missing> {
    fn new() -> Self {
        RequestBuilder(String::new(), PhantomData)
    }

    fn url(self, url: String) -> RequestBuilder<HasUrl> {
        RequestBuilder(self.0 + &url, PhantomData)
    }
}

impl RequestBuilder<HasUrl> {
    fn method(self, method: String) -> RequestBuilder<Ready> {
        RequestBuilder(self.0 + &method, PhantomData)
    }
}

impl RequestBuilder<Ready> {
    fn build(self) -> Request {
        // Build the request
    }
}
```

This combination provides:

- Strong type safety through distinct types
- Self-documenting APIs that guide users through correct usage
- Enhanced IDE support with state-appropriate method suggestions
- Zero runtime cost since all checks happen at compile time

By leveraging the Typestate pattern, you can encode state transitions into Rust's type system, letting the compiler enforce correct usage and preventing many common bugs at compile time rather than runtime.
